# PROVENANCE: data/derived/single_feature_bedroc.csv

**Producer:** hand-run — no automated reproducer in `reproduce/`.

**How it was made.** Exported by an ad-hoc cell in an earlier version of what is now
`notebooks/28_single_feature_bedroc.ipynb` (was NB 16 in the pre-flatten numbering).
The cell iterated every MD + ligand-chem column, computed the per-target BEDROC α=20
under both "global direction from panel" and "oracle direction per target" conventions,
and stamped `is_lig_chem` for each row so the downstream ranking could exclude the
ligand-chemistry rows on request.

**Columns:** `feature, is_lig_chem, direction, panel_bedroc_global_dir, panel_bedroc_oracle_dir, targets_beat_GBSA`.

**Consumers.**
- `notebooks/28_single_feature_bedroc.ipynb` — reads it to render the single-feature bar chart.
- `notebooks/29_rank_fusion_deployable.ipynb` — reads the top-K ranking to build the
  LOTO K-fusion sweep.
- `notebooks/30_family_stratification.ipynb` — reads the top-5 MD features to run the
  per-family recomputation on the recovered 9-target panel.
- `reproduce/canonical_baselines.py` (via `hardened_claim_b.csv`) — does NOT depend on
  this file directly, but the ranking convention here is what selects
  `lig_partial_q_sum` as `top_feature_naive_9T`.

**Status.** Live consumer inputs exist — cannot delete without breaking NB 28/29/30.
Values were computed on the 8-target subset (pre-4A5S-recovery); the 9-target
recompute lives in memory inside NB 30 (Section 3), never persisted.

**To regenerate on demand.** Open `notebooks/28_single_feature_bedroc.ipynb`, run
through the single-feature sweep cell, then export the resulting `sf` DataFrame
with `sf.to_csv(DERIVED / 'single_feature_bedroc.csv', index=False)`. To promote
this to a proper reproducer, wrap the sweep loop into
`reproduce/single_feature_bedroc.py` and call it from `reproduce/README.md`.
